h2 { "Calls, correctness and editor tools" }

Ordinary suspending function calls use task-local resumable call frames with a separate memory budget. Calling a function no longer consumes a task-admission slot by itself; explicit tasks retain scheduler admission limits. Ownership, errors, faults, cancellation and child settlement are preserved across suspension and early return.

Buffer reuse after cancelled native file work now synchronizes with retired readers. Live snapshots still trigger copy-on-write. Shared compiler type facts and capability prerequisites add no runtime type-category dispatch.

Qualification includes native CPU execution across all 20 directed platform pairs, CPU/Metal sanitizers where supported, and physical Linux CUDA/Vulkan correctness on RTX 5090. Vulkan Intel ASan/UBSan exits cleanly. NVIDIA retains the unsuppressed external loader/D-Bus leak tracked in issue 298; a same-path pre-refactor control reproduces it, and it is not reported as a clean NVIDIA LeakSanitizer pass. CUDA ASan/TSan, Vulkan TSan, Windows sanitizer limitations and cross-target sanitizer restrictions remain explicit.

The updated VS Code extension and native language server cover the new keywords, primitives, traits, associated members and library APIs. Language services include diagnostics, completion, hover, navigation, references, rename and formatting. Install extension 0.2.0 from this release and select the matching native server. Other LSP editors use the same server over standard input/output; installed native tools and the VSIX do not require Node.js or npm.

