h2 { "Migrate from 0.99.2" }

table {
  row = ["Previous source or behavior", "0.99.3"],
  row = ["int8…int64, uint8…uint64, float32, float64", "i8…i64, u8…u64, f32, f64"],
  row = ["int, uint, float type aliases", "Choose an explicit width such as i64, u64 or f64."],
  row = ["Bool", "bool; unit uses the literal ()."],
  row = ["Final expression implicitly returns a result", "Write return value, including in anonymous functions."],
  row = ["Returning a main result prints it", "Call print/println explicitly; return unit or ExitStatus."]
}

Unconstrained integer/decimal literals still infer i64/f64. Larger types retain PascalCase. Unit functions may finish naturally, use bare return, or return (). Expression-valued if, match and loops remain useful as operands, including after return.

Mutable function parameters use the explicit mut contract; owned resources use own when ownership transfers. Aliases and task readers constrain in-place writes. copy explicitly copies storage rather than changing the meaning of assignment.

h2 { "Download and install" }

Choose your build host's package from GitHub Releases, verify its SHA-256 sidecar and keep the complete directory together. Package names and platform baselines are unchanged. macOS 26+ Apple silicon uses signed/notarized Metal, CPU-only or editor-only DMGs. Metal compilation requires Xcode 26.2 and the pinned Metal Toolchain component. Linux x86-64/ARM64 requires glibc 2.39 or newer; modern Raspberry Pi systems use 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS Trixie. Windows 11 x86-64/ARM64 requires matching Visual Studio C++ tools and the Windows SDK.

Linux x86-64 also provides CUDA/Vulkan archives; Windows GPU archives remain experimental. Host packages bundle private compiler/linker tools and CPU runtime data for all five destinations. System development libraries and GPU drivers remain prerequisites. Cross-compilation covers CPU programs. Keep a generated GPU application's executable and PTX/SPIR-V resources together. Standalone CUDA compilers do not require the CUDA toolkit, NVRTC or cuBLAS; execution requires a compatible NVIDIA driver supporting PTX 9.2.

Download kalandra-native.vsix from the same release and use Extensions: Install from VSIX. Select its native language server using Kalandra Native: Server Path. The extension version is 0.2.0; the native compiler and language server report 0.99.3.

